# Ground truth annotations for offline evaluation graders.
#
# Each line is a JSON object keyed by example_id (integer from DSQAExample.example_id).
# Fields you can annotate:
#
#   plan_rubric.must_cover          — list of key concepts the plan must address
#                                     (the only field that cannot be auto-derived)
#   tool_pattern.reference_tool_call_count — expected number of tool calls from
#                                     an expert run (overrides the category average)
#
# All other rubric fields (min_steps, max_steps, complexity, must_have_synthesis,
# must_use, may_use, data_type) are auto-derived from answer_type and problem_category
# and do not need to be listed here unless you want to override them.
#
# How to find example IDs:
#   Run the helper cell in 06_offline_evaluation.ipynb (Section 0: Browse Questions)
#   or run:  python data/langfuse_upload.py --samples 20 --category "Finance & Economics"
#   then inspect the Langfuse dataset items for their example_id values.
#
# Format example (replace with real IDs from your dataset):
# {"example_id": 1001, "plan_rubric": {"must_cover": ["concept A", "concept B"]}, "tool_pattern": {"reference_tool_call_count": 4}}
